    "acknowledgment": "The author and book histologic resolution in biopsies repeated editors acknowledge the prior contribution of following a clinically successful gluten-free Krysta Deitz diet trial. PATHOLOGIC FINDINGS •Histologic examination of jejunal biopsy specimens from affected dogs reared on a wheat-containing diet reveals partial villus atrophy and accumulation of intraepithelial lymphocytes. •Jejunal abnormalities improve following gluten withdrawal, but recur with gluten challenge. TREATMENT Treatment is on an outpatient basis. Avoid diets containing gluten (wheat, rye, barley, triticale, brewer’s yeast, and wheat starch) for the life of the animal. MEDICATIONS DRUG(S) OF CHOICE Folate (0.5–2 mg PO q24h for 2–4 weeks) if serum folate concentration is markedly subnormal (<4μg/L). CONTRAINDICATIONS/POSSIBLE INTERACTIONS N/A FOLLOW-UP Consider periodic assay of serum folate (q6–12 months) if initially decreased. MISCELLANEOUS ABBREVIATIONS •TLI = trypsin-like immunoreactivity Washabau RJ, Day MJ, eds., Canine and Feline Gastroenterology. St. Louis, MO: Elsevier, 2013, p. 403. intestine. In: Ettinger SJ, Feldman EC, Côté E, eds., Textbook of Veterinary Internal Medicine, 8th ed. St. Louis, MO: Elsevier, 2017, p. 1547. et al. Characterization of paroxysmal gluten-sensitive dyskinesia in Border terriers using serological markers. J Vet Intern Med 2018, 32(2):775."
